<h1 class="article-title">Navigation Safety Improved in the Karosta Canal at the Port of Liepāja</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>With the installation of ship-protection guide structures beside the O. Kalpaks swing bridge in the Karosta Canal, the Liepāja SEZ Authority has completed the final component of EU Cohesion Fund project No. 6.1.1.0/17/I/004, “Restoration of the Hydraulic Structures of the Karosta Canal and Northern Entrance”. The project restored safe navigation through the northern port entrance, renovated the Karosta Canal breakwaters, dismantled Berth 1 and installed lighting in the canal, meeting its objective of improving navigation safety.</p><p>The Karosta Canal is a technically complex waterway with heavy ship traffic. Its relatively narrow fairway, entrance breakwaters, protruding shoreline and especially the structures of the swing bridge complicate vessel movements and increase the risk of maritime accidents. Installing protective guide structures reduces the risk of a ship colliding with the bridge because of human error or technical problems, threatening vessel safety and traffic through the canal. The works cost EUR 2,370,583.00 in total, including EUR 1,349,192.20 from the EU Cohesion Fund.</p><p>The guide structures comprise two separate installations on the southern and northern sides. Each consists of 15 steel tubular piles driven into the canal bed and connected by horizontal pipes. Each installation is approximately 98 metres long and fitted with navigation signs and composite sliding surfaces. They are designed to protect the swing bridge against collisions with vessels up to the following dimensions:</p><ul><li>Deadweight capacity — 25,000 tonnes;</li><li>Length overall (LOA) — 180 metres;</li><li>Beam — 28 metres;</li><li>Draught — 8.6 metres.</li></ul><p>Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis emphasises the importance of preventing incidents like the 2006 collision, when the tanker “Anna” struck the northern section of the Karosta bridge, cutting traffic across the canal for several years and forcing Karosta residents to take a much longer route to the city centre. The project has substantially improved maritime safety and navigation. “Previously the maximum permitted vessel width for entering the Karosta Canal safely was 24 metres; now it is possible to accommodate wider ships.”</p><p>Ivo Koliņš, Head of the Liepāja SEZ Authority’s Port Infrastructure Projects Department, says that completion of the ship guide structures near the Karosta swing bridge marks the end of EU Cohesion Fund project No. 6.1.1.0/17/I/004. Alongside the guide structures, the project included reconstruction of Berth 1, restoration of Karosta Canal breakwaters and installation of night lighting in the Karosta and Pilots’ canals. Together, these measures have significantly improved navigation safety.</p><p>During the EU funds programming period 2014–2020, the Liepāja SEZ Authority implemented a broad programme to develop shared port infrastructure, placing particular emphasis on navigation safety through the following projects:</p><ul><li>“Development of Land Access Roads to the Port of Liepāja”, total eligible costs: EUR 5,853,368.57;</li><li>“Improvement of Water Infrastructure at the Port of Liepāja”, total eligible costs: EUR 30,287,974.95;</li><li>“Restoration of the Hydraulic Structures of the Karosta Canal and Northern Entrance”, total eligible costs: EUR 4,013,191.10.</li><li>These projects directly support the goals of Latvia’s National Development Plan 2014–2020. Multimodal transit corridors comprising roads, railways and ports play an important part in national economic development: they increase the volume of goods transported, encourage new processing and service industries, and help Latvia and its residents benefit from the country’s advantageous geographical location.</li></ul></div>
